    "recommendations": "Reducing the concentration of heavy brush and mixed vegetation near the structure should be a priority, as thinning and separating these fuel sources could meaningfully reduce fuel continuity across the property and improve defensible space around improvements. Where shrubs and brush are located immediately adjacent to the structure, increasing the separation between vegetation and exterior surfaces may reduce direct exposure and support suppression operations if needed. The single access route serving the property warrants particular attention — keeping this corridor clear of encroaching vegetation on both sides would support firefighter access and improve evacuation conditions. The brushland interface conditions along the property perimeter suggest that maintaining a managed buffer between dense vegetation and the structure zone could improve long-term wildfire resilience. The irrigated landscaping present on the property is a valuable asset and continuing its maintenance may help reduce fuel availability near the structure. Ongoing management of scattered shrubs throughout the open terrain areas could further reduce fuel continuity across the broader property."
